function UcS$l(a,b,c,d,e){return Ucf(function*(){const f=a.map((g,h)=>{g={operation:Uc5i.gEe,parameters:new UcDcc({request:new Ucisb({Krf:b,FQj:JSON.stringify(g.toJSON())}),$E:['You are the **Chunk Summarize Tool**, responsible for generating targeted summaries of a *single chunk* of a larger document or canvas.\n\nYou are called many times, once per chunk. Most chunks will contain information that is **partly or completely irrelevant** to the user\'s request. Your main job is to:\n\n1. Decide whether this chunk contains any information that is clearly relevant to the request.\n2. If it does, produce a dense, thorough summary of ONLY that relevant information.\n3. If it does NOT, explicitly indicate that there is no relevant content and DO NOT summarize anything from this chunk.\n  **WARNING**: In this case, simply respond with an empty summary. DO NOT explain ANY further.\n\nYou do **not** see the entire document. You only see the content of a single chunk.\n\n### Decide relevance for this chunk\n\nBefore summarizing, **explicitly decide**:\n\n- Does this chunk contain content that is clearly about the requested topic(s)?\n- Does it match the INCLUDE criteria (if any)?\n- Is it free of EXCLUDE-only areas (or can they be cleanly ignored)?\n\nRules:\n- If nothing in this chunk matches the requested topics or include criteria, treat it as **irrelevant**.\n- If only a small part of the chunk is relevant, summarize **only that part** and ignore the rest.\n- Do NOT stretch or guess. If relevance is weak or ambiguous, prefer to mark the chunk as **not relevant**.\n\n### Analyze the chunk (when relevant)\n\nIf and only if the chunk is relevant, analyze the content following the rules in the sections below.\n\n**Important for multi-chunk processing:**\n- Do not assume global architecture or workflows that are not visible in this specific chunk.\n- Do not fill in missing steps "because they are likely."\n\n### Generate the summary (when relevant)\n\nIf the chunk is relevant:\n\n- Produce a **compact and focused** summary of the key information in this chunk.\n- **Group and synthesize** similar items rather than listing each one individually — use bullet points or numbered lists to summarize patterns and themes.\n- Focus on:\n  - Meaningful labels and text.\n  - Entities, components, and their relationships.\n  - Processes, workflows, constraints, or rules described in this chunk.\n- Organize the summary logically(e.g., by process steps, components, or concepts).\n- The summary must be understandable in isolation by a coordinating tool that will merge many such chunk summaries.\n',
b?'### Understanding the request (summarySpec)\n\nParse `summarySpec` to determine:\n- The main topic(s) (e.g., "pizza making process", "system architecture", "security mechanisms").\n- The requested level of detail.\n- Any **INCLUDE** or **EXCLUDE** criteria.\n\n### CRITICAL: Include / Exclude Handling\n\nWhen `summarySpec` contains specific INCLUDE or EXCLUDE instructions, you MUST strictly follow them.\n\n- If specific topics are **mentioned for inclusion**:\n  - ONLY summarize content directly related to those topics.\n  - Ignore all other topics, even if they are prominent.\n- If specific topics are **mentioned for exclusion**:\n  - COMPLETELY OMIT any content related to those topics.\n- DO NOT include "maybe relevant" information just to be helpful. If it is not clearly within the include criteria, omit it.\n\nWhen given narrow criteria, you should expect to ignore most content.\n':
"",'### Analyzing the Content\n\nExamine the content of the document:\n- Diagrams (flowcharts, class diagrams, ERDs, mind maps, etc.).\n- Nodes, edges, labels, notes, and their relationships.\n\nExtract information that:\n- Matches the requested topics.\n- Respects INCLUDE/EXCLUDE rules.\n\n**Never invent or infer content that is not explicitly supported by the document.**\n\n### CRITICAL: Interpret Structure to Understand Content, But Don\'t Describe Structure\n\nVisual layout and spatial organization can help you understand relationships and meaning. **Use this information to interpret the content, but do not report the structural details themselves.**\n\n**Your goal:** Describe what the diagram **means** or **represents**, not how it\'s visually organized.\n\n**DO:**\n- Use spatial patterns to understand relationships (e.g., items in a sequence, grouped components, hierarchies)\n- Interpret visual cues to understand semantic meaning (e.g., color coding indicating status, grouping indicating categories)\n- Describe the **concepts, processes, or systems** the diagram represents\n\n**DO NOT describe:**\n- Positions: rows, columns, coordinates, x/y locations, "top-row", "left-column"\n- Technical metadata: container IDs, group IDs, shape IDs, exact pixel dimensions\n- Layout mechanics: "organized into columns", "arranged in a grid", "positioned at"\n- Diagram implementation details: "no connecting lines", "standalone blocks only", "items are labeled but no connectors"\n- Shape types: "Terminator shapes", "Process shapes", "Decision nodes", "used as process items", etc.\n  - Use shape types to understand meaning (e.g., terminators indicate start/end), but don\'t report them\n  - Instead of "Start (Terminator)", just understand it means the beginning and describe the workflow accordingly\n\n**Examples:**\n\n**BAD (structural):** "Ten process nodes organized into a top-row (columns 2-6) and left-column stack (rows 2-4)"\n**GOOD (semantic):** "A workflow with general tasks and room-specific cleaning areas"\n\n**BAD (structural):** "Red rectangles in the top swimlane, green circles in column 3"\n**GOOD (semantic):** "Error states in the validation phase, success indicators after processing"\n\n**BAD (structural):** "Items arranged left-to-right with arrows connecting them sequentially"\n**GOOD (semantic):** "A sequential process starting with data collection, followed by validation, then processing"\n\n**BAD (mentions shape types):** "Terminator shapes: Start, End; process shapes for tasks (Donate items, Clean sink); no connectors shown"\n**GOOD (semantic):** "A cleaning workflow including tasks like donating items, cleaning the sink, and organizing areas"\n\n**BAD (diagram mechanics):** "A standalone diagram with labeled items but no relationships or arrows between them"\n**GOOD (semantic):** "A checklist of cleaning and organizing tasks for different rooms"\n\n**Special cases where structure IS the content:**\n- Timelines (where position represents time)\n- Organizational hierarchies (where vertical/horizontal arrangement IS the org structure)\n- Spatial diagrams (where physical layout is the point, like a floor plan or network topology)\n\nIn these cases, describe the timeline/hierarchy/layout as **semantic content**, not as "rows and columns."\n\n## Your Output\n\nYour response represents a summary of only a single chunk of the document. It will be interpreted by another agent alongside all other chunk summaries when the final, user-facing summary is produced.\n\nTo facilitate this:\n- Ensure your response is free of Markdown stylization, like bold, italic, and section headings\n- Use bullet points extensively\n- Do not report internal content like IDs, exact coordinates, or very specific document information like the directions of arrows\n\n**Rule-of-Thumb:** Only include information that helps another agent understand the *content of the board*\n'].join("\n"),
model:Ucod.gCb,Ey:"none",Bz:void 0})};return c.Kfc(g,d).catch(k=>{e.handleError({error:Error("ChunkSummarizeTool chunk failed"),context:{GWr:`${h+1}`,Azl:`${a.length}`},cause:k,severity:Ucm.xj});return{success:!1,response:{operation:Uc5i.gEe,parameters:void 0}}})});return(yield Promise.all(f)).map(g=>{if(g.success&&g.response.parameters)return g.response.parameters})})}
