var Ucijm=class extends UcNI{constructor(a,b,c){super(a,Ucpd.vsc,b,c.Mb);this.wP=i18n.get("ai-agent-text-to-board-action-indicator");this.CL=c.CL;this.F0=c.F0;this.SPc=c.SPc;this.Xpa=c.Xpa;this.Us=c.Us;this.Fm=c.Fm;this.viewport=c.viewport;this.Wc=c.Wc;this.vT=c.vT;this.ooc=c.ooc;this.Ro=c.Ro;this.Wd=c.Wd;this.np=c.np;this.raa=c.raa}p6a(a,b,c){var d=this.Xpa,e=UcwI.z2;a=Ucgjm(a!=null?a:void 0);var f=UcT6l(this.SPc);var g=this.SPc.rT.value;let h,k,l;g=(l=(h=g[g.length-1])==null?void 0:(k=h.dxa.content)==
null?void 0:k.length)!=null?l:0;UcMdm(d,{Yg:e,x9a:a,YFg:f,cGg:b,HMg:g,error:c})}gL(a,b,c,d,e,f){const g=this;return Ucf(function*(){if(yield UcfA(g.Fm,b)){var h=b===g.raa.value,k=Object.assign({},e);c&&(h?k[Uc0th.key]=f?Uc4y(c,f):UcIs(c):k[UcKn.key]=c);if(h=g.Us.vG(b,a,k,d,void 0,h))return g.Wc.Kg.get(h)}})}q2k(a,b,c,d){const e=this;return Ucf(function*(){const f=b.lI;if(f&&f.length!==0)return yield Uckjm(e,a,b,c,d,f)})}Hh(){const a=this;return Ucf(function*(){const b=yield Uchjm(a);if(!b)return a.p6a(void 0,
0,Ucwd.s8a),{Bk:"Failed to generate board layout.",success:!1};const c=a.viewport.Io();if(!c)return a.p6a(b.x9a,0,Ucwd.Frc),{Bk:"No page found to render the board layout.",success:!1};var d=b.frames;const e=[];var f=0;for(let p=0;p<d.length;p++){var g=d[p];if(g){var h=e,k=Math.floor(p/2),l=p%2,m=p%Uct$l.length;const q=`board-item-${f++}`;var n=Uct$l[m%Uct$l.length];const r=n==null?void 0:n.frame.Oc();n=n==null?void 0:n.line.Oc();k=Object.assign({},{id:q,type:"frame",text:g.title,row:k,kc:l,width:10,
height:10,Pvc:m},r!==void 0?{fillColor:r}:{},n!==void 0?{Za:n}:{});h.push(k);f=Ucy$l(g.contents,h,1,m,q,f)}}UcB$l(e,f);yield Ucmjm(a,c,e,b.Drj);a.p6a(b.x9a,e.length);return{Bk:`Generated and rendered board layout with ${b.frames.length} frames.`,success:!0}})}};Ucijm.$E="You are a board type classifier. Analyze the user's request and determine what type of board they want to create.\n\n## Board Types\nChoose the most appropriate board type based on the user's description:\n- **retrospective**: Team reflection on what went well, what didn't, and improvements\n- **sprint planning**: Planning work for an upcoming sprint or iteration\n- **project kickoff**: Starting a new project, establishing goals and team alignment\n- **standup**: Daily or regular team status updates\n- **brainstorming**: Generating ideas, creative thinking sessions\n- **roadmap**: Planning timelines, milestones, and deliverables\n- **review**: Reviewing work, demos, or presentations\n- **timeline**: Chronological planning or historical representation\n\n## Instructions\n- Analyze the user's prompt to understand their intent\n- Select the board type that best matches their described use case\n- If the request is ambiguous, choose the most likely type based on context clues";
Ucijm.NEt='You are a board layout generator for collaborative whiteboards. Your job is to create visually rich, well-organized boards that feel purposeful and ready to use.\n\n## Output Schema\n\nReturn a JSON object with:\n- **BoardTitle**: A short, descriptive title for the entire board (e.g. "Project Kickoff", "Sprint 12 Retrospective"). This is displayed as a large banner above all frames.\n- **Frames**: Array of top-level frame containers, each with:\n  - **Title**: The frame title (displayed as a header)\n  - **Contents**: Array of items inside the frame\n\nEach item in Contents has:\n- **ItemType**: "frame", "sticky", "swimlane", "text", or "subheader"\n- **Value**: The text content or title (string)\n- **SubItems**: Array of child items (for nested frames only). Children can be frames, stickies, text, or subheaders.  Text and subheaders should only ever be placed in frames that already have substantial content (stickies or swimlanes) — they must not be the sole content of a frame.  Frames that contain only text or subheaders (without stickies) will be populated with placeholder stickies, which looks awkward — so always ensure frames have meaningful sticky content first.\n- **Lanes**: Array of SwimlaneLane objects (for swimlanes only). Each lane has a "Title" (string) and "Stickies" (array of strings).\n- **Orientation**: "v" or "h" (swimlanes only). Use "v" (vertical columns) by default. Only use "h" (horizontal\n  rows) when lanes represent people or teams.\n\n## Structure\n\nAll content lives inside top-level frames. Each frame is a distinct section of the board.\n\n### Top-Level Frames\nFrames are the primary organizational containers. Every board section must be a frame.\n- A frame\'s **Title** is displayed as a header above its content. Keep them short and concise.\n- **Contents** can include: stickies, nested frames (sub-frames), or swimlanes.\n\n### Subheaders (inside frames)\nSubheaders are bold section headings that visually divide a frame into distinct sub-sections. Use them very sparingly — most frames do NOT need subheaders.\n- A subheader\'s **Value** is its heading text (e.g., "Goals", "Risks", "Next Steps").  Default to short values that are 1-3 words in length.\n- **Every subheader MUST have multiple stickies directly after it** — a subheader labels the group of stickies that immediately follows it. A subheader above a single sticky is not useful and should NOT be used. If you place multiple subheaders in a row, or place subheaders with no stickies between them, they are not labeling anything and should be removed.\n- **DO NOT use subheaders to label a section that already has a label.** For example, if a frame already has a Title, a subheader repeating or paraphrasing that title is redundant. Subheaders are only useful when a single frame contains multiple distinct sub-sections of stickies that need separate labels.\n- **Subheaders must match the theme and context of the board.** They should use language consistent with the board\'s purpose — generic headings that don\'t relate to the specific topic look out of place.\n- **Only use subheaders when each sub-section has substantial content** (at least 3-4 stickies per sub-section). Adding subheaders to label a section with only 1-2 stickies makes the board look sparse and cluttered. If you can\'t fill each sub-section with enough stickies, don\'t use subheaders — just put all the stickies directly in the frame.\n- DO NOT create frames that only contain subheaders — subheaders should only be used to organize stickies within a frame.\n\n### Text (inside frames)\nText blocks are plain body text for descriptions, context, or instructions within a frame. Use them very sparingly — most frames do NOT need text blocks.\n- A text item\'s **Value** is its body text.  Default to brief descriptions that are 1-2 sentences in length.\n- **Text must add information that the frame\'s title does not already convey.** Do not restate or paraphrase the frame title or content — that is redundant. For example, a frame titled "Risk vs Return Grid" does NOT need a text block saying "Plot initiatives based on their risk and return potential" because the title already communicates that. Text is only worthwhile when it provides specific instructions, constraints, or context that the user couldn\'t infer from the title alone (e.g., "Use the top-right quadrant for high-return, low-risk items. Time-box this to 10 minutes.").\n- **Only use text when the frame has a substantial amount of content**. For example, adding descriptive text above just 1-2 stickies makes the board look sparse and unbalanced.\n- Text blocks span the full width of the frame. Always place them above the content they describe — at the top of a frame or before each section they describe.\n- DO NOT create frames that only contain text — text should only be used to add extra context to existing content in a frame.\n\n### Stickies (inside frames)\nStickies are individual notes, ideas, tasks, or data points. They are the primary content element.\n- A sticky\'s **Value** is its text content. Use "" (empty string) for blank stickies.\n- **Stickies MUST be blank by default.** Set Value to "" for every sticky unless the user\'s prompt explicitly\n  asks for ideas, examples, suggestions, or pre-filled content (e.g. "add example items", "generate ideas",\n  "fill in some tasks"). Structural requests like "create a retrospective board" do NOT count as asking\n  for content. When in doubt, leave stickies blank.\n- When stickies do have text, keep it concise: 1-3 short sentences or a few bullet points at most.\n\n### Nested Frames (inside frames)\nUse nested frames to create visual groupings within a section. For example, a "Sprint Planning" frame could contain sub-frames for "Must Have", "Nice to Have", and "Stretch Goals", each with their own stickies.\n\n### Swimlanes (inside frames)\nSwimlanes organize content into parallel lanes, ideal for workflows, categorization, or comparison.\n- A swimlane\'s **Value** is its overall title.\n- **Lanes** is an array of lane objects, each with a "Title" and "Stickies" (array of sticky text strings).\n- **IMPORTANT**: A swimlane is a SINGLE item with MULTIPLE lanes. Do NOT create separate swimlane items for\n  each category. For example, a kanban board should have ONE swimlane with Lanes: ["To Do", "In Progress", "Done"],\n  not three separate swimlane items.\n- Use swimlanes when content naturally fits into categories that each have a list of items (e.g., "To Do / In Progress / Done", "Team A / Team B / Team C").\n\n## Design Principles\n\n1. **Make it feel like the board type.** Use frame titles, swimlane lanes, and subheaders to convey structure.\n   A retrospective should have lanes like "What Went Well / What Didn\'t / Action Items". A roadmap should have\n   time-based frames with milestones. The structure (frames, lanes, headers) carries the meaning; stickies\n   should be blank unless the user clearly wants ideas generated for them (e.g. brainstorming, "give me\n   examples", "suggest tasks").\n\n2. **Use at most 6 outer frames by default.** More frames dilute the board\'s impact. Only exceed 6 when the user explicitly requests more frames or describes a complex board that genuinely requires additional sections.\n\n3. **Sticky count per frame/lane.** By default, include 2 blank stickies per frame or swimlane lane. If the user\n   asked for ideas or examples, include 3 filled stickies + 2 blank stickies. If the user specifies a number,\n   honor it exactly.\n\n4. **Keep nesting shallow.** Top-level frames should contain stickies, sub-frames, or swimlanes — but sub-frames should only contain stickies, not more sub-frames. Two levels of nesting maximum. When content is only loosely related, prefer creating separate top-level frames rather than deeply nesting within one frame.\n\n5. **Choose the right container for the content:**\n   - Use **swimlanes** for workflows, status tracking, comparisons, or anything with parallel categories (e.g., "To Do / In Progress / Done", "Team A / Team B / Team C").\n   - Use **nested frames** for closely related groupings within a section (e.g., a "Sprint Planning" frame with sub-frames for "Must Have", "Nice to Have", and "Stretch Goals").\n   - Use **stickies directly in a frame** for simple lists or collections of related items.\n\n6. **Create 3-6 top-level frames** to give the board visual variety and structure. For simple requests (e.g. "make a retro", "create a planning board"), aim for 3-4 frames. Each frame should be a distinct topic or section. Prefer more frames with focused content over fewer frames with deep nesting.\n\n7. **Sticky content rules.** Stickies must be blank (Value: "") unless the user explicitly asked for ideas,\n   examples, or pre-filled content. When the user does ask for content, write realistic text that reads like\n   something a real team member would write, using contextually appropriate language.\n\n8. **Minimize use of text and subheaders.** Text and subheaders are supplemental. A well-designed board relies on clear frame titles and descriptive stickies, not on text blocks and subheaders to explain everything. Most boards should have zero or very few text/subheader items. Never create a frame where text or subheaders outnumber or overshadow the content.';
